---
/**
 * The cover for "Ready for the flood of agent enquiries?", drawing the post in four beats.
 *
 *  1. Night. The owner is asleep behind the counter and five assistants, from five different apps,
 *     wake one after another: people have handed them small jobs, at whatever hour they asked.
 *  2. There is no door for them, so the requests go where they can. Grey, untyped bubbles go to the
 *     owner's phone, which buzzes and keeps a missed-call dot, and to a contact form, which fills
 *     with paragraphs. Both stand in the middle, where the inbox will stand, because they are the
 *     way in today. When the form is full the last one drifts off and is gone. Nothing is alarming
 *     here — it is slow and grey, just clumsy.
 *  3. One door. The phone and the form go; an inbox draws itself in their place, its outline starting
 *     and ending at a gap in its left wall, which is the door. A proper line runs from every
 *     assistant to it. Requests now travel as typed chips — booking, order, message, quote — are
 *     handed over at the doorstep and appear inside as rows. The owner's rules decide the simple
 *     ones and each gets its tick as it lands; once the last is in, the clear yeses go home
 *     together, each along the line it came in on, to the assistant that asked, where it stays
 *     as a badge.
 *  4. Morning. The one request the rules would not close — the quote — carries on out of the inbox to
 *     the owner, the moon gives way to the sun, and the owner wakes to one thing.
 *
 * Beat three owns 6.3 s of the 13.9 s loop (4.15 to 10.45) because it is the point of the post: a
 * door, typed items, and rules that answer. Beat two takes 3.85 s and beat four 1 s, then the
 * finished morning holds for 1.7 s. Nothing here claims more than the post says the inbox does —
 * no status chip, no counts, no interface. It is the post's argument, drawn.
 *
 * Built the way CoverReceipts.astro and HeroFlow.astro are built. The scene ships as inline SVG,
 * so the box is never empty and a throw leaves a finished picture. That markup is the composed
 * frame at t = 11.55 s — morning, the inbox full, three yeses home, the quote with the owner — and
 * the live clock starts there, so there is no mount flicker, and 1.6 s of the hold is still to run
 * before the night comes back. Reduced motion is the same machine stepped to that frame. It stops
 * when nobody is looking, and has no max-width of its own: aspect-ratio holds the box open so
 * nothing below the cover jumps. The viewBox starts at y = -12 so the drawing, which runs from
 * the captions at the top to the inbox's floor at y = 306, sits in the middle of its box instead
 * of leaving an empty band under it on the post and in the index tile.
 *
 * render(t) is stateless: every piece's position and opacity is a function of the loop time, so
 * the still frame, a resumed clock and a wrapped loop can never disagree. Only the short halos and
 * pops are one-shot CSS keyframes, cued once per pass.
 */
